	Architecture

RVM models a whole board, not just an instruction set. Everything here exists
so an unmodified Debian kernel and userland will run on it, on a desktop and on
a phone, in well under 5 MB.

	+--------------------------------------------------------+
	| main.c, ui/          CLI, raw mode terminal            |
	+--------------------------------------------------------+
	| vm/vm.c              machine assembly, run loop        |
	| vm/sbi.c             SBI: timer, console, IPI, reset   |
	+--------------------------------------------------------+
	| cpu/     mmu/        bus/                              |
	| core+CSR Sv39/48/57  RAM and MMIO dispatch             |
	+--------------------------------------------------------+
	| devices/  uart  clint  plic  virtio-mmio  virtio-blk   |
	+--------------------------------------------------------+
	| loader/   ELF64, raw blob, FDT builder                 |
	+--------------------------------------------------------+
	                   libc and libm only

Every arrow points down. cpu/ has never heard of virtio. It reaches memory
through bus_load and bus_store function pointers and traps otherwise. That is
why the test suite can drive the CPU with a hand built bus and no VM at all;
see tests/fixtures/harness.c.


Memory map
----------

One hart, one address space. dtb/rvm.dts and src/loader/fdt.c describe the same
machine.

	0x02000000  CLINT       64 KB    msip, mtimecmp, mtime at 10 MHz
	0x0c000000  PLIC        64 MB    64 sources, one M and one S context
	0x10000000  UART0       256 B    ns16550a, IRQ 10
	0x10001000  virtio      8 x 4 KB slots 0 to 7, IRQs 1 to 8
	0x80000000  RAM         8 MB to 8 GB, default 1 GB

src/rvm.h is the single source of truth for those numbers. The FDT builder and
the .dts are both derived from it and test_fdt_blob checks the result.


CPU
---

cpu_exec32() in src/cpu/cpu.c is one switch over the whole RV64GC opcode space.

Compressed instructions are normalised, not duplicated. c_expand() in
src/cpu/compressed.c turns any 16 bit instruction into its exact 32 bit
equivalent and hands that to the same execute path. The decompressor emits real
encodings rather than an intermediate form, which keeps it auditable: --trace
shows you the expanded instruction.

Every non branching path falls through to one epilogue:

	c->pc = taken ? npc : (c->pc + (c->last_insn_len ? c->last_insn_len : 4));

Any case that returns early, meaning the AMO helper, the FP helper, mret and
sret, has to advance pc itself or re-arm npc. Three separate bugs in the first
working version were exactly this, and all three looked like an instruction
executing forever.

Corner cases are guarded before the host operation, not after. x86 raises
SIGFPE on INT64_MIN / -1 and on INT32_MIN % -1, so the spec's defined result
has to be selected before the C division runs. The same applies to MULHSU,
where rs2 is unsigned: converting the u64 to __int128 preserves its value,
which is exactly the required zero extension.

csr.c holds the CSR file. mstatus.SD is never stored. It is computed on read
from FS and XS, because it is architecturally read only and a stored copy goes
stale the moment an FP instruction retires.


MMU
---

mmu_translate() is one function used by fetch, load and store. It takes the
effective privilege and mxr and sum as arguments rather than reaching into the
CPU, so the tests can drive Sv48 walks directly.

Sv39, Sv48 and Sv57 share one loop parameterised by level count. Superpages are
supported including the misaligned PPN check that rejects a megapage whose
PPN[0] is non zero. A and D are managed by hardware, which the spec permits and
which avoids the trap storm a software managed implementation causes on Linux.
The TLB is direct mapped on the page number with a generation tag, so
sfence.vma invalidates by bumping the tag instead of walking 256 entries.

Two permission rules that are easy to get wrong. R=0,W=1 is reserved and
faults. W=1,X=1 is legal, and treating it as reserved faults on every writable
and executable page Linux maps.


Devices
-------

Each device is a load/store pair over an address and size, attached with
bus_attach(). Nothing in bus.c knows what a UART is.

UART is an ns16550a with a real 16 byte FIFO, DLAB divisor registers and an
interrupt line. uart_set_sink() redirects transmitted bytes: the CLI sends them
to stdout, the harness sends them to a buffer it can assert on.

CLINT advances mtime from the host clock at a 10 MHz guest timebase and
compares it against mtimecmp to produce MTIP. clint_tick() returns those bits.
clint_mtime() returns the counter. They are not the same value.

PLIC has priorities, per context enables, pending bits, threshold and the claim
and complete handshake.

virtio-mmio lives in virtio.c and device types plug in behind it. virtio_blk.c
implements the split virtqueue protocol against a host file with pread and
pwrite, so a guest write really reaches the disk and
test_virtio_blk_roundtrip can read the file back to prove it.

Devices raise interrupts through a raise(ud, irq, level) callback that the VM
wires to plic_raise(). The direction matches the memory one: devices point down
at the bus, never up at the CPU.


Loader and device tree
----------------------

loader_load_elf() parses ELF64 program headers and copies PT_LOAD segments to
their physical addresses, rejecting anything outside RAM. loader_load_blob()
handles raw images at a given base.

fdt.c builds the DTB in C. That is deliberate: dtc is not installed everywhere
and not on Android at all, and a VM that cannot describe its own machine cannot
boot. dtb/rvm.dts is the same machine written by hand for editing, and
test_fdt_blob walks the generated blob to check the nodes Linux needs are
present with the right reg cell order.


Run loop
--------

vm.c assembles the machine and owns the loop.

	while (running) {
	    if (irq_dirty || insns % 256 == 0) {
	        pump_input();           /* a read() syscall */
	        refresh_interrupts();   /* a clock_gettime() */
	    }
	    switch (cpu_step()) {
	        STEP_OK:       continue;
	        STEP_TRAP:     continue;   /* the trap already moved pc */
	        STEP_ECALL_M:  sbi_call(); /* SBI is policy, not CPU */
	        STEP_WFI:      idle();     /* refresh first, then sleep */
	        STEP_FAULT:    report();
	        STEP_SHUTDOWN: return;
	    }
	}

pump_input() and the clint refresh are syscalls. Running both on every retired
instruction cost several times more than the instruction itself, so they run
every 256 instructions and immediately whenever a device moves an interrupt
line. irq_raise() sets the dirty flag, which means console input gets picked up
at once, since the UART's irq callback is irq_raise. The WFI path refreshes
explicitly, because going to sleep on a stale nothing-pending is the one place
a throttle could hang the guest. clint_load() advances mtime itself on an
explicit MMIO read, so the clocksource stays smooth between refreshes.

The CPU has no idea SBI exists. It traps on ecall from M-mode and the run loop
interprets it. That keeps cpu.c free of policy and lets sbi.c be tested on its
own.


What is deliberately not here
-----------------------------

No JIT for cold code. Blocks are interpreted; only blocks that have run 50
times or more get cached, and that is PLAN step 12. The cache is keyed on guest
PC and falls back to the interpreter on any miss, so correctness never depends
on it.

No DMA engine. virtio transfers are memcpy between the guest buffer and the
host. A scatter gather engine would be faster and much harder to reason about.

No threads in the VM. One hart, one thread, no locks. The UI polls; it does not
share the CPU struct.

No heavy dependencies. libc and libm. On Android that is what keeps the APK
small: no AndroidX, no Material, no Compose, no WebView. The terminal is a
Canvas and the framebuffer is a SurfaceView.
